.TH CJSON_CLONE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cjson_clone \- deep-copy a DOM subtree
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cjson.h>

cjson cjson_clone(cjson node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns a fully independent copy of the subtree under node.  The
function duplicates every string and copies every array and every
object together with its contents.  The clone uses the same allocator
as the source, which is the allocator that the root node keeps, and
each object of the copy keeps its members in the order of the source
object.

The function refuses a subtree that nests more than 500 levels deep,
where each array or object is one level, in any combination, and a
scalar adds none.  It reports such a subtree in the same way as an
allocation failure.  The limit is the same as the limit of the
parser, so the function can clone any tree that parsed; only the
construction API, through cjson_list_push() and
cjson_dictionary_set(), can build a deeper tree, because that API has
no depth bound while a parsed document does.  This is a policy limit,
not a guess about the stack size of the calling thread: the function
walks the nesting with an explicit worklist on the heap, so a tree at
the limit costs the same small, fixed amount of native stack as a
flat one.  An ordinary tree never comes near this depth.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A new root node, which the caller owns, on success.  NULL when the
allocation fails, or when node nests more than 500 levels deep.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_destroy (3),
.BR cjson_dictionary_first (3)
